    "result_text": "All 3 tests pass and the build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented address normalization applied whenever an owner is created, with minimal structural impact \u2014 two files touched and no change to any complex method.\n\n**New file \u2014 `util/AddressNormalizer.java`:** a small, self-contained utility (zero surrounding class complexity) with one static `normalize(String)` that trims, collapses internal whitespace, upper-cases, and expands `ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE` per word.\n\n**`model/Owner.java`:** changed `setAddress` to delegate to `AddressNormalizer.normalize(...)` (one line + one import). This mirrors the existing idiom where `setTelephone` delegates to `E164.toE164` and `setEmail` lower-cases, so the setter stays CC=1.\n\n**Why this satisfies the whole spec without touching more code:**\n- Normalization runs whenever an owner is created (the mapper builds the entity via `setAddress`), and the stored/returned `address` is the normalized form.\n- **Required-field check:** an all-whitespace address normalizes to `\"\"`; the existing DTO pattern `.*\\S.*` (equivalent to \"blank after normalization\", since normalization never removes non-whitespace characters) rejects it with 400.\n- **Household duplicate detection** (`DuplicateHouseholdException`) and the **shared household id** (`Household.idFor`) both read `owner.getAddress()`, which now returns the normalized value \u2014 so both automatically compare the normalized form with no edits to those classes.\n\nTest run: `Tests run: 3, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.",
